{"id": "671ac922-f85f-49bc-9074-04bff804b7b4", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "0x800B0101 is CERT_E_EXPIRED, meaning a certificate is outside its validity period relative to the system clock or file timestamp—common on old restored images with a stale clock or expired root certs. The fix is to check and correct the system date/time/timezone, then update the root certificate store and relevant servicing stack.

For Server 2012 R2 specifically, I should consider the SHA-2 code signing update (KB4474419) and servicing stack update (KB4523206), but also note that 2012 R2's ESU coverage ended October 13, 2026, so Windows Update may no longer serve patches. Since this is an AWS EC2 instance, I should also check Amazon Time Sync Service and the RealTimeIsUniversal setting, and consider using SSM to inspect the instance's current state before recommending fixes.

